WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 24 Student Video
Unit 6, Lesson 4: Authority

1
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:21.260
Baseball, the American pastime. Stolen bases, home runs, grand

2
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:26.220
slams, hot dogs and nachos. These are some of the things that make a baseball game

3
00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:26.760
fun.

4
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.560
Part of the experience, of course, is yelling at the umpires.

5
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.000
And let's face it, they're going to make a lot of mistakes.

6
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.620
They're human. When they make a bad call or they miss a call, we let them have it.

7
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.220
But what if there were a perfect umpire?

8
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:46.400
This umpire got calls correct one hundred percent of the time.

9
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.900
You know with modern technology today we can almost do this.

10
00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:55.520
We can tell if a pitch is a strike or a ball, or if a runner is safe or out.

11
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.360
If an umpire had that knowledge would we listen to this umpire?

12
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.540
The answer is of course we would.

13
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.920
Now a call from our perspective might seem like they got it wrong but given that

14
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.880
this umpire is correct 100% of the time, we would always know that the umpire got

15
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.480
the call right. Human beings differ wildly on all sorts of social, ethical,

16
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.980
scientific, and theological issues.

17
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.160
But what if there was an umpire that was always correct, especially on spiritual

18
00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:29.140
issues? This umpire never got it wrong.

19
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.020
Would we listen to that person?

20
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.740
Some time ago, I went with my wife and my pastor to meet a group of skeptics,

21
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.200
atheists, and agnostics. Towards the end of the meeting, my pastor asked a really

22
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.980
interesting question. He said to this group of people who don't believe in God, he

23
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:52.240
said if there were a God, would his opinion be more valuable than yours?

24
00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.440
And to me, the answer seems obvious.

25
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.460
If there's a God who spoke all of creation into existence and knows everything,

26
00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.440
then of course his opinion is more valuable than mine.

27
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.880
This group of people hesitated and eventually they said no.

28
00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.900
Even if there were a God, his opinion would not be more important or authoritative

29
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.160
than theirs.

30
00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.060
This gets to the heart of the matter of authority in our world.

31
00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:23.740
Who do we trust? Now we would trust a perfect umpire and this umpire would have

32
00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:28.000
authority because he or she would never make the wrong call.

33
00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:29.960
Well, the same is true with God.

34
00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.000
If there is a God, and this God by definition is self-existent, eternal, perfectly

35
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.880
good, loving, and knows everything, then by definition, this God is gonna

36
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:47.400
make the right call. Now it might seem from our perspective that God has made the

37
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.440
wrong call, but if we stopped and focused on the character of God, we would know

38
00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:55.660
that the problem is not with the call, that the problem is with our perspective.

39
00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:07.490
Recently I was invited to participate in a public conversation with a former pastor

40
00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:12.430
who's become an atheist and he asked some good questions such as how could a loving

41
00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:17.530
God send somebody to hell or why would God have his chosen people in the Old

42
00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:22.750
Testament engage in warfare? These are thoughtful questions that every Christian

43
00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:28.150
needs to wrestle with. But rather than answer his particular questions, as important

44
00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:31.750
as they are, I wanted to frame the conversation differently.

45
00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:38.210
I said that any adequate worldview has to account for certain big features in

46
00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:43.830
reality. Any adequate worldview has to explain certain big questions we have about

47
00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:48.490
life, such as why is the universe so exquisitely fine-tuned for life?

48
00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:52.830
What best explains the origin of information and DNA?

49
00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:58.510
And what worldview can account for the origin of things like objective beauty,

50
00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:04.790
morality, and free will? The reality is that atheism can't answer any

51
00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:09.150
of the big questions of life. But Christianity can.

52
00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:15.050
What this means is that if Christianity is true, then we can trust that God is the

53
00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:19.770
authority for all of life. Are there some tough questions that remain like this

54
00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:24.810
pastor asks? Yes. but we can trust that God exists and God is good and he has

55
00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:30.210
reasons why even if he doesn't always fully reveal those reasons to us.

56
00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:35.730
Jesus believed that God's word was authoritative for all of life.

57
00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:40.290
That's why when he was asked tough questions, he often responded by saying, have you

58
00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.030
not heard or have you not read?

59
00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:47.390
Appealing to the Old Testament because he believed that God's word was reliable

60
00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:50.810
and authoritative in everything that it taught.

61
00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.920
If God does exist, then his opinion is supreme, even if we don't understand it.

62
00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:07.360
Like the umpire we mentioned who never missed a call.

63
00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:13.820
God never makes mistakes. The question is, will we listen to him?

64
00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.220
This is not an apologetic issue or argument.

65
00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.840
This is just a deep question we need to wrestle with personally in our own lives.

66
00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:25.840
Who are we going to look to as the authority?

67
00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:31.520
The only way we can be right with God and experience the abundant life that Jesus

68
00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.820
promised is by following God's guide for our lives as found in the scriptures.

69
00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:41.800
Many people in our world will say that the Bible is antiquated or God is a cosmic

70
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:47.600
killjoy, but this isn't true. God's commandments and teachings exist for our good.

71
00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:53.740
God has our best in mind. So regardless of how you feel or

72
00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:59.100
how things appear to be in your life, the question is will you trust God as the

73
00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.640
ultimate authority in your life?

74
00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:06.200
It's hard to imagine a question more important than this.

